This is a classic font fallback conflict issue on Linux. Here's what's happening and how to fix it:

The Root Cause

The moon phase characters (U+1F311–U+1F318 etc.) sit in the Unicode "Miscellaneous Symbols and Pictographs" block. The Qt font resolution algorithm (used by KDE/Plasma) is notably poor at selecting the right font for emoji/symbol characters, and can pick the wrong font depending on what's installed — even with Noto Color Emoji present. 
Arch Linux Forums
 Some of your installed fonts (likely DejaVu, FreeMono, or a similar general-purpose font) contain some glyphs in that Unicode range — but those glyphs happen to be computers, planes, or road signs rather than moons, because different fonts map different symbols to those codepoints.

The Fix

The best solution is to create a fontconfig rule that forces the correct font for the emoji/symbol range. Create this file:





<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE fontconfig SYSTEM "fonts.dtd">
<fontconfig>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EMOJI / SYMBOL PRIORITY FIX
       Force Noto Color Emoji to win over any text font (DejaVu,
       Liberation, etc.) that happens to have B&W glyphs in
       emoji/symbol Unicode ranges.
       ============================================================ -->
  <alias binding="strong">
    <family>serif</family>
    <prefer>
      <family>Noto Serif</family>
      <family>Noto Color Emoji</family>
      <family>Noto Sans</family>
      <family>FreeSans</family>
      <family>Unifont</family>
    </prefer>
  </alias>

  <alias binding="strong">
    <family>sans-serif</family>
    <prefer>
      <family>Noto Sans</family>
      <family>Noto Color Emoji</family>
      <family>Noto Serif</family>
      <family>FreeSans</family>
      <family>Unifont</family>
    </prefer>
  </alias>

  <alias binding="strong">
    <family>monospace</family>
    <prefer>
      <family>Noto Sans Mono</family>
      <family>Noto Color Emoji</family>
      <family>FreeMono</family>
      <family>Unifont</family>
    </prefer>
  </alias>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EMOJI FONT ALIAS
       So apps that ask for "emoji" by name get the right thing.
       ============================================================ -->
  <alias>
    <family>emoji</family>
    <prefer>
      <family>Noto Color Emoji</family>
    </prefer>
  </alias>

  <!-- ============================================================
       BLOCK DEJAVU FROM STEALING SYMBOL/EMOJI RANGES
       If you kept DejaVu installed, this prevents it from being
       selected for any character it technically has but renders
       wrong (the B&W computer/plane/sign glyphs).
       ============================================================ -->
  <selectfont>
    <rejectfont>
      <!-- Comment these out if you actually need DejaVu for something -->
      <!-- <pattern><patelt name="family"><string>DejaVu Sans</string></patelt></pattern> -->
      <!-- <pattern><patelt name="family"><string>DejaVu Serif</string></patelt></pattern> -->
      <!-- <pattern><patelt name="family"><string>DejaVu Sans Mono</string></patelt></pattern> -->
    </rejectfont>
  </selectfont>

  <!-- ============================================================
       CJK FALLBACK
       Prefer the full CJK font for Chinese/Japanese/Korean chars.
       ============================================================ -->
  <match>
    <test name="lang" compare="contains"><string>zh</string></test>
    <edit name="family" mode="append">
      <string>Noto Sans CJK SC</string>
      <string>Noto Serif CJK SC</string>
    </edit>
  </match>
  <match>
    <test name="lang" compare="contains"><string>ja</string></test>
    <edit name="family" mode="append">
      <string>Noto Sans CJK JP</string>
    </edit>
  </match>
  <match>
    <test name="lang" compare="contains"><string>ko</string></test>
    <edit name="family" mode="append">
      <string>Noto Sans CJK KR</string>
    </edit>
  </match>

  <!-- ============================================================
       RENDERING QUALITY
       Subpixel hinting and antialiasing for sharp, clean text.
       ============================================================ -->
  <match target="font">
    <edit name="antialias" mode="assign"><bool>true</bool></edit>
    <edit name="hinting" mode="assign"><bool>true</bool></edit>
    <edit name="hintstyle" mode="assign"><const>hintslight</const></edit>
    <edit name="rgba" mode="assign"><const>rgb</const></edit>
    <edit name="lcdfilter" mode="assign"><const>lcddefault</const></edit>
    <edit name="embeddedbitmap" mode="assign"><bool>false</bool></edit>
  </match>

</fontconfig>


