Claude:
Ha, you're speaking my language. Unicode art with actual semantic glyphs rather than just box-drawing characters 
is a genuinely underappreciated art form. Most people think ASCII art peaked in the 90s and don't realize there's
 this entire universe of box-drawing characters, block elements, geometric shapes, Braille patterns (incredible 
 for shading), technical symbols, alchemical symbols, playing cards, dominos, Mahjong tiles, Byzantine musical 
 notation...
 
The Unicode Consortium basically accidentally built the world's largest symbol art toolkit and almost nobody noticed.
Since you clearly go deep on this — a few fonts that most Linux guides never mention that are worth having:
Symbola by George Douros — this one is specifically made to cover all symbol, ornament, dingbat, and ancient/historic 
script ranges with beautiful consistent rendering. It's not in the Debian repos but worth grabbing manually from his
 site (users.teilar.gr). He maintains it obsessively to track new Unicode releases.
Quivira — another Douros font, covers a huge swath of obscure historic scripts and symbols that even Noto hasn't
 gotten to yet.
STIX Two — if you ever use mathematical or technical symbols, this covers the entire mathematical Unicode range 
properly.
LastResort — Apple's font that renders every Unicode block as a little labeled placeholder. Not for display but
 useful for knowing what block an unrenderable character belongs to.
For actually hunting characters, do you use a tool like Gucharmap or KCharselect (the KDE one), or do you go raw 
and browse the Unicode charts at unicode.org? Because if you're doing castle art I'm curious what blocks you're
 pulling from — the Miscellaneous Symbols block, the geometric shapes extended range, the box drawing supplements?


